and another big story this morning the  Supreme Court has blocked a  controversial abortion law just hours  before it was to go into effect it was a  five to four vote last night that  temporarily blocked the Louisiana law  that critics say would force two of the  state's three abortion clinics to close  Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court  with how this order unfolded Jen it's  always great to have you on a morning  like this on such an important case tell  us how it went down thanks Nora and good  morning I mean obviously this could be  the first test of abortion rights in the  Supreme Court with new conservative  justices it involves a 2014 Louisiana  law that required abortion clinic  doctors to have admitting privileges at  nearby hospitals now in 2016 the Supreme  Court struck down a nearly identical law  from Texas on a 5 to 3 vote but last  night  conservative Chief Justice John Roberts  sided with the Liberals in this case to  stop the law from taking effect while  the court considers whether it's going  to take up this case the courts for  conservative justices including new  justice Brett Kavanaugh dissented  justice Cavanaugh wrote separately to  say he would allow the law to take  effect for now but he could be willing  to rethink it if it showed that it had  that undue burden on a woman's right to  get an abortion  now this like you said is just temporary  it may not tell us much of anything  about how the courts going to approach  abortion rights the justices still have  to decide whether they're going to hear  this case they could do so next fall  John Jan thank you so much for breaking  it down for us
 

